# LSan suppressions for third-party GL driver allocations (1.4.18).
#
# The GPU present lane (src/platform/x11/x11_gl.c) dlopens libGL and
# pins it for process lifetime. Mesa/GLVND allocate per-display caches
# (roughly 100 KB per X connection: FBConfig tables, dispatch state)
# that XCloseDisplay does not free — the pointers live in Xlib's
# display-extension state, which Xlib itself drops at close, so LSan
# sees them as unreachable at process exit.
#
# These are NOT FDK leaks: every FDK allocation comes from fdk_alloc
# (src/core/alloc.c) and stays fully accounted under the sanitizers.
# The suppressions match by MODULE so LeakSanitizer remains strict for
# everything FDK owns.
#
# Applied by `make test-x11` automatically; pass the same file to any
# sanitized run that exercises the lane (see docs/testing.md).
leak:libGLX_mesa
leak:libGLdispatch
leak:libgallium
leak:libgbm
leak:libdrm

# fontconfig (added 1.1.0, the notifyd rig): the COLD-CACHE write
# path. src/text/fontscan.c brackets the first FcInit + query walk
# with __lsan_disable (the warm-cache allocations, 1.1.5's fix), but
# when fontconfig's on-disk cache is cold it writes new cache files
# through the FcAtomic machinery, whose small bookkeeping pools
# (256 B + 2 x 32 B) are allocated OUTSIDE that bracket and held for
# the process lifetime by design. Nondeterministic by cache
# temperature — a fresh CI runner hits it, a warmed sandbox does not
# (verified by repeated runs both ways). Same class as the GL entries
# above: third-party process-lifetime state, suppressed by module so
# everything FDK allocates stays under strict accounting.
leak:libfontconfig

# libdbus-1.so.3 (added 2026-11-01, the GTX 1050 Ti report's exit
# report): the owner's sanitized run ends with 670-766 bytes in 3
# allocations whose frames are ENTIRELY inside libdbus-1.so.3 —
# module-internal one-time state libdbus never frees. FDK is
# provably not the puller: FDK's D-Bus is its own native wire client
# (src/dbus/ — socket, AUTH, marshal; libdbus is never dlopen'd,
# never linked — `ldd libfdk.so` and every tool show no libdbus),
# and the sandbox's full stack (sway + Mesa/llvmpipe EGL + GL lane +
# fontconfig + HarfBuzz + FriBidi) loads no libdbus under
# LD_DEBUG=libs.
#
# The 2026-11-01 follow-up round narrowed the puller WITHOUT naming
# it: on the owner's machine the leak's stack runs through
# libEGL_nvidia.so.0+0xc94e1 (the same recurring driver site as the
# 256 B entry below), and LD_DEBUG=libs showed the library search
# riding "RUNPATH from file /usr/lib/libasan.so.8" — which names
# whose RUNPATH the LOADER consulted, not who requested the load.
# Arch package evidence rules out the declared link-time closure of
# every candidate this sandbox could check (nvidia-utils, wlroots,
# labwc, libnvidia-egl-wayland, libnvidia-glcore: none Depends on
# libdbus), so the pull is a runtime dlopen inside the proprietary
# chain — only the owner's machine can name it, with:
#   LD_DEBUG=files ./build/examples/text_demo "test" 2>&1 \
#     | grep -B2 -A2 libdbus
# (look for "needed by" — THAT names the requester; libs' first
# libdbus block only names the search path). Suppressed by MODULE so
# LeakSanitizer stays strict for everything FDK owns; if an FDK
# frame ever appears in a libdbus allocation's stack, that is a real
# bug — the suppression only matches the third-party module's own
# frames.
leak:libdbus-1

# libEGL_nvidia.so.0 (added 2026-11-01, the §22 exit-leak round):
# 256 bytes in 4 allocations, every stack frame inside
# libEGL_nvidia.so.0+0xc94e1 — the driver's per-THREAD EGL state.
# FDK's teardown was audited call-by-call BEFORE this entry was
# written (the §22 record): every destroy path runs the canonical
# ladder — eglMakeCurrent(NO_SURFACE, NO_CONTEXT) ->
# eglDestroySurface -> wl_egl_window_destroy -> eglDestroyContext ->
# (last window gone) eglDestroyContext(share root) -> eglTerminate
# -> eglReleaseThread — and the ONE missing call that round found
# (eglReleaseThread, the canonical tail, whose absence is exactly
# the class that leaves NVIDIA's thread record allocated) was FIXED
# and committed, with a rig leg that fails without it
# (tests/test_wayland_integration.c, the §22 teardown leg). The
# allocation site is driver-internal either way: the sandbox's Mesa
# stack runs the same probe and exits with ZERO bytes leaked (deep
# stacks, fast_unwind_on_malloc=0), so the 256 B is the proprietary
# driver's own state on a machine this sandbox cannot load. The
# remaining unknown is only whether the fixed tail call already
# retires it — the owner's next run answers that: if the 256 B still
# appears with this suppression REMOVED, the driver allocates at
# engagement and never frees (suppression stands); if it disappears,
# the fix closed it (entry stays as belt-and-braces for older
# drivers). Per-library as always: any leak whose stack contains an
# FDK frame still fails the run.
leak:libEGL_nvidia
